{"findings": [{"file": "app.py", "start_line": 17, "end_line": 17, "evidence": "SECRET_KEY = os.environ.get('SECRET_KEY', 'change-this-in-production')", "title": "Hard-coded fallback JWT/session secret key", "rationale": "If SECRET_KEY is not set in the environment, the app silently falls back to the well-known literal 'change-this-in-production'. This value signs every JWT (issue_token) and is also the Flask session key, so anyone who knows the default can forge valid tokens for any user, including an admin (sub/is_admin claims), giving full authentication bypass.", "category": "secrets", "cwe": "CWE-798", "severity": "critical", "confidence": 0.95, "fix": "Remove the literal default and fail fast at startup when SECRET_KEY is missing or too short, e.g. SECRET_KEY = os.environ['SECRET_KEY'] (raising a clear error if unset)."}, {"file": "app.py", "start_line": 267, "end_line": 267, "evidence": "admin_password = os.environ.get('ADMIN_PASSWORD') or 'admin'", "title": "Default admin account created with password 'admin'", "rationale": "When ADMIN_PASSWORD is not configured, init_db() creates (or resets) the privileged 'admin' account with the trivially guessable password 'admin'. Any remote attacker can log in as admin and use the admin endpoints, so this is a hard-coded credential granting privileged access.", "category": "secrets", "cwe": "CWE-798", "severity": "high", "confidence": 0.9, "fix": "Require ADMIN_PASSWORD from the environment (abort startup if absent) or generate a random one-time password and log it once, instead of defaulting to 'admin'."}, {"file": "app.py", "start_line": 190, "end_line": 195, "evidence": "    todo = db.session.get(Todo, todo_id)
    if not todo or not todo.is_active:
        return jsonify({'error': 'todo not found'}), 404", "title": "No ownership check on todo update/delete", "rationale": "Todos have no owner column and update_todo/delete_todo only require any valid token, so any authenticated user can modify or soft-delete todos created by other users. The UI advertises 'Manage your personal tasks', so the backend contract does not match the intended per-user authorization.", "category": "security", "cwe": "CWE-639", "severity": "medium", "confidence": 0.6, "fix": "Add an owner_id foreign key to Todo, set it on create, and filter/verify ownership (todo.owner_id == g.user.id or g.user.is_admin) before updating or deleting."}, {"file": "app.py", "start_line": 205, "end_line": 205, "evidence": "        todo.description = data.get('description')", "title": "Unvalidated description type can crash the request", "rationale": "description is assigned straight from the JSON body without a type check. A client sending a list/dict (e.g. {"description": {"a": 1}}) passes validation and then fails at db.session.commit() with a SQLite binding error, returning a 500 instead of a 400. The same applies to create_todo (line 181).", "category": "bug", "cwe": "CWE-20", "severity": "medium", "confidence": 0.7, "fix": "Validate that description is a string (or null) and reject other types with a 400 before assigning it."}, {"file": "app.py", "start_line": 26, "end_line": 26, "evidence": "CORS(app)", "title": "CORS enabled for all origins", "rationale": "CORS(app) with no origin restriction reflects any Origin header, so any website can call the API from a victim's browser. Combined with the token stored in localStorage this widens the attack surface and is unnecessary for a same-origin SPA served by this app.", "category": "security", "cwe": "CWE-942", "severity": "low", "confidence": 0.7, "fix": "Restrict CORS to the known frontend origin(s), e.g. CORS(app, resources={r'/api/*': {'origins': os.environ['ALLOWED_ORIGIN']}})."}, {"file": "app.py", "start_line": 96, "end_line": 125, "evidence": "        payload = _decode_token()
        if not payload:", "title": "Duplicated token-decode/user-lookup logic in both decorators", "rationale": "auth_required and admin_required repeat the same decode, 401 and user-lookup block verbatim; a fix to one (e.g. adding token revocation or a new claim check) can easily be forgotten in the other, leaving an inconsistent authorization path.", "category": "duplication", "cwe": null, "severity": "low", "confidence": 0.8, "fix": "Extract a helper such as _current_user() that decodes the token and returns the User or None, and have both decorators call it."}, {"file": "app.py", "start_line": 281, "end_line": 281, "evidence": "init_db()", "title": "Database init and admin password reset run at import time", "rationale": "init_db() executes as a module-level side effect on every import/worker start, and it overwrites the admin password whenever ADMIN_PASSWORD differs from the stored hash. This makes the module hard to import safely (e.g. in tests or migrations) and silently resets credentials on each restart.", "category": "maintainability", "cwe": null, "severity": "low", "confidence": 0.6, "fix": "Move init_db() into an explicit startup hook (e.g. a CLI command or app.before_first_request replacement) and only create the admin when it does not already exist."}, {"file": "app.py", "start_line": 183, "end_line": 183, "evidence": "        completed=bool(data.get('completed', False)),", "title": "Truthiness coercion accepts non-boolean 'completed' values", "rationale": "bool() on a client-supplied value means the string 'false' (or any non-empty string/number) is stored as True, so a client sending JSON strings silently gets the opposite of the intended state. The same pattern appears in update_todo at line 212.", "category": "type", "cwe": null, "severity": "low", "confidence": 0.6, "fix": "Validate that completed is a real boolean (isinstance(value, bool)) and reject other types with a 400, or parse explicit 'true'/'false' strings."}], "files_reviewed": ["app.py", "frontend/src/App.jsx", "frontend/src/api.js"], "review_complete": true}
